>
> On Sun, Oct 12, 2014 at 12:29 PM, Gleki Arxokuna <
> gleki.is.my.name@gmail.com> wrote:
>
>>
>>>
>>>> How to describe two-way doors, bridges and streets?
>>>>
>>>
>>> "relselfa'a" (vs. "pavyselfa'a")?
>>>
>>
>> With corresponding places deleted with zi'o? won't that be too bulky?
>>
>
> For example? Not sure what you mean.
>


cripu =3D x1 is a bridge/span over/across x2 between x3 and x4 [unordered,
typically destination first].

Now if you say {farna} derivative can do what cripu3 and cripu4 can do then
why do we have cripu3 and cripu4?
When using cripu3/4 we can't even be sure where is destination due to
"typically".

And most bridges are two way bridges. Using cripu place structure looks
like a nightmare here:

if the bridge A is between B and C then it is
{abu cripu be fi by bei cy be'o je cripu be fi cy bei by be'o}
which can be "compressed" into

{abu ve se te sexiveipa.ereve'o te se ve cripu by. cy.}
Not really a compression since it doesn't save syllables.

Also I don't understand how to use {relselfa'a} since it doesn't manage
cripu3/4 that continue to exist possible creating conflicts with
{relselfa'a}.




> mu'o mi'e xorxes
